WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.152
By the end of the module, you should be able to structure multi-step SQL with CTEs, trace a recursive query,

00:00:06.152 --> 00:00:10.352
choose the correct ranking function, analyze sequences with LAG and LEAD,

00:00:10.352 --> 00:00:13.672
and write explicit frames for running and moving calculations.

00:00:13.672 --> 00:00:17.647
You should also understand the difference between GROUP BY and PARTITION BY.

00:00:17.647 --> 00:00:19.987
The goal is not only to run the syntax.

00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:23.057
You should be able to explain the row meaning at every stage.

00:00:23.057 --> 00:00:26.182
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:26.182 --> 00:00:30.587
an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event.

00:00:30.587 --> 00:00:37.802
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:38.987
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:43.672
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:00:43.672 --> 00:00:47.602
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:47.602 --> 00:00:50.160
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
